1
00:00:01,710 --> 00:00:03,504
Hey, will you steam my uniform next?

2
00:00:03,712 --> 00:00:06,507
Interesting.
Do you recall this conversation?

3
00:00:06,715 --> 00:00:09,051
"Leonard, wanna go halfsies
on a steamer?"

4
00:00:09,301 --> 00:00:11,887
"No, Sheldon, we don't need a steamer."

5
00:00:13,347 --> 00:00:16,058
Looks like that rumpled
chicken's come home to roost.

6
00:00:17,059 --> 00:00:19,186
Hi. Here are the makeup
sponges you asked for.

7
00:00:19,394 --> 00:00:21,980
-Oh, thanks, I thought I had more.
-Hmm.

8
00:00:25,484 --> 00:00:28,320
Damn, you've got more makeup than I do.

9
00:00:28,529 --> 00:00:32,031
You've got better makeup than I do.
Yeah, I'm borrowing this.

10
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:35,369
Hey, hey, hey,
this is my Comic-Con makeup.

11
00:00:35,577 --> 00:00:39,331
I love you, but there are some things
a man doesn't share with his girlfriend.

12
00:00:39,998 --> 00:00:41,708
That's a wise policy.

13
00:00:41,917 --> 00:00:44,670
I once borrowed my sister's makeup
for a costume contest...

14
00:00:44,878 --> 00:00:46,588
...got a terrible case of pink eye.

15
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:50,217
Yeah, well, luckily, I was going
as a zombie, I won second place.

16
00:00:51,593 --> 00:00:53,220
You guys just went to Comic-Con.

17
00:00:53,428 --> 00:00:56,431
That was San Diego Comic-Con.
This is Bakersfield Comic-Con.

18
00:00:57,224 --> 00:00:59,351
-Is that better?
-Well, it's a lot smaller.

19
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:00,811
It's about the comic books.

20
00:01:01,019 --> 00:01:03,939
The way these conventions used to be
before they went Hollywood.

21
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:06,608
So to answer your question,
no, it's not better.

22
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:09,278
Then why are you going?

23
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:11,405
It's a comic book convention.

24
00:01:11,613 --> 00:01:13,532
Like pizza or particle accelerators...

25
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,951
...even the stinky ones, still pretty good.

26
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:19,371
Well, you guys have fun.
I guess I'll see you Sunday.

27
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:21,748
-Yeah. Oh, hang on a second.
-Okay.

28
00:01:21,957 --> 00:01:23,792
Hold this.

29
00:01:28,755 --> 00:01:31,967
-What was that for?
-To show people when they don't believe me.

30
00:01:58,535 --> 00:02:02,122
This is the best. You have booze with
breakfast on Tuesday, you got a problem.

31
00:02:02,372 --> 00:02:04,708
You do it on the weekend,
you got brunch.

32
00:02:05,208 --> 00:02:06,835
Sheldon doesn't believe in brunch.

33
00:02:07,002 --> 00:02:08,378
The Bakersfield Expedition

34
00:02:08,545 --> 00:02:11,632
Can't stand a table where one person's having
an omelet and another is having a sandwich.

35
00:02:12,507 --> 00:02:15,010
That's not exhausting at all.

36
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:17,763
[PHONE BEEPS]

37
00:02:17,971 --> 00:02:19,014
Oh.

38
00:02:19,222 --> 00:02:23,644
It's Leonard. He says they're on the road
and headed for Bakersfield at warp speed.

39
00:02:24,144 --> 00:02:27,147
Maybe it's the mimosa,
but I'm gonna give the kid an LOL.

40
00:02:29,149 --> 00:02:30,442
Cute how excited they are.

41
00:02:30,651 --> 00:02:33,403
You should have seen Howard
sewing his costume all week.

42
00:02:33,904 --> 00:02:35,322
When did Howard learn to sew?

43
00:02:35,572 --> 00:02:39,618
When he was a little boy, every couple of
months he'd have to let his mom's pants out.

44
00:02:42,871 --> 00:02:45,248
I don't understand
why they go to these conventions.

45
00:02:45,457 --> 00:02:48,543
The four of them work at a major university,
they're all smart...

46
00:02:48,752 --> 00:02:51,129
...how can they be into something
for 12-year-olds?

47
00:02:51,338 --> 00:02:55,133
I don't mind it. I think Howie's
just in touch with his inner child.

48
00:02:55,342 --> 00:02:57,176
Although when he's in Batman pajamas...

49
00:02:57,427 --> 00:03:00,430
...sometimes it feels like
I'm touching his inner child.

50
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:06,853
It's probably because they were bullied.
In a world where you can't fight back...

51
00:03:07,104 --> 00:03:09,106
...superheroes provide
meaningful wish-fulfillment.

52
00:03:09,314 --> 00:03:12,984
Hmm. Now I feel bad for picking on
all those kids.

53
00:03:13,192 --> 00:03:16,195
Although if Danny Biffle
didn't want to eat a mouthful of dirt...

54
00:03:16,446 --> 00:03:19,116
...he shouldn't have shown up
to school wearing a bow tie.

55
00:03:20,492 --> 00:03:22,619
I've never even read a comic book.
You guys?

56
00:03:22,828 --> 00:03:23,829
PENNY: Mm-mm.
-No.

57
00:03:24,037 --> 00:03:25,997
It's such an important part of their lives.

58
00:03:26,205 --> 00:03:29,126
-Maybe we should try reading some.
-Seriously?

59
00:03:30,085 --> 00:03:32,170
The comic book store isn't far from here.

60
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:34,965
That is an amazing idea.

61
00:03:35,173 --> 00:03:37,216
Okay, how many of these have I had?

62
00:03:43,098 --> 00:03:45,517
I think you should turn on the GPS.

63
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:46,810
It is on.

64
00:03:47,018 --> 00:03:49,438
But the turn-by-turn voice option isn't on.

65
00:03:49,646 --> 00:03:52,941
I know I'd feel more safe
if you turn on the turn-by-turn voice option.

66
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:55,402
I love the turn-by-turn voice option.

67
00:03:56,319 --> 00:03:59,197
Has it really only been 10 miles?

68
00:04:00,157 --> 00:04:02,993
I'm turning it on, but just to shut you up.

69
00:04:03,744 --> 00:04:09,166
GPS [IN SHELDON'S VOICE]: Leonard,
bear left and continue on Interstate 210.

70
00:04:11,084 --> 00:04:14,212
Ooh, sounds like that fellow
knows what he's talking about.

71
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:17,215
I'd put on my listening ears if I were you.

72
00:04:17,674 --> 00:04:20,719
-What did you do?
-I found a hack online.

73
00:04:20,927 --> 00:04:24,723
I was able to upload MP3 recordings
of my voice to your GPS.

74
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:27,934
-That is so cool.
-Counterpoint, no, it's not.

75
00:04:29,519 --> 00:04:33,732
GPS:
Continue on Interstate 210 for five miles.

76
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,735
Here's an interesting fact
about Interstates:

77
00:04:38,153 --> 00:04:39,905
-Really?
-Shh!

78
00:04:40,113 --> 00:04:41,281
He said it's interesting.

79
00:04:42,365 --> 00:04:45,076
GPS:
Interstates are numbered as follows:

80
00:04:45,284 --> 00:04:49,039
Even-numbered routes run east and west.

81
00:04:49,539 --> 00:04:53,210
Odd-numbered routes run
north and south.

82
00:04:53,585 --> 00:04:59,049
Three-digit route numbers
indicate bypasses or spurs.

83
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:04,095
Look, Leonard, there's a bridge.
Drive off it.

84
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,682
You know, we're not that far
from Vasquez Rocks.

85
00:05:07,891 --> 00:05:10,936
Oh, they shot a lot of
Star Trek episodes out there.

86
00:05:11,186 --> 00:05:13,104
We got our costumes in the trunk...

87
00:05:13,313 --> 00:05:15,690
...we could go there
and have a little photo shoot.

88
00:05:15,899 --> 00:05:19,402
Great idea. I haven't had
a carbohydrate in two weeks.

89
00:05:19,611 --> 00:05:23,740
These cheekbones need to be in front of a
camera before I eat a pretzel and they're gone.

90
00:05:24,825 --> 00:05:27,452
-It sounds fun.
-Oh, smashing.

91
00:05:27,702 --> 00:05:29,955
Now, Leonard,
do you know how to get there?

92
00:05:30,205 --> 00:05:33,041
-No.
-Yeah, well, luckily, someone in the car does.

93
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:35,293
GPS:
Recalculating.

94
00:05:36,586 --> 00:05:39,464
While we're waiting,
do you know which president...

95
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:43,969
...signed the
Interstate Highway System Act into law?

96
00:05:44,219 --> 00:05:47,806
The answer coming up in 14 miles.

97
00:05:49,266 --> 00:05:52,602
None of you will get it. It's Eisenhower.

98
00:06:06,116 --> 00:06:08,118
Why are they staring?

99
00:06:09,119 --> 00:06:11,288
Who cares, just soak it in.

100
00:06:13,372 --> 00:06:15,125
Hello, boys.

101
00:06:16,251 --> 00:06:18,295
Oh, hey.

102
00:06:21,256 --> 00:06:23,508
Could you please stop staring?
They're just girls.

103
00:06:23,717 --> 00:06:26,261
It's nothing you haven't seen
in movies or in drawings.

104
00:06:28,263 --> 00:06:30,682
-Hey, Stuart.
-What brings you guys here?

105
00:06:30,891 --> 00:06:33,476
We were looking for a recommendation
about comic books.

106
00:06:33,685 --> 00:06:36,980
Well, I recommend you don't
open a store and sell them.

107
00:06:37,564 --> 00:06:40,984
We were wondering why the guys
like this stuff, so we thought we'd try it.

108
00:06:41,192 --> 00:06:43,320
Oh, okay.
What do you think you might be into?

109
00:06:43,528 --> 00:06:45,989
Superhero? Fantasy?
Graphic novels? Manga?

110
00:06:46,197 --> 00:06:48,867
I swear I will turn a hose on you.

111
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:55,832
What kind of comics do the guys like?

112
00:06:56,041 --> 00:06:59,377
Um, a little bit of everything.
Mostly superhero stuff.

113
00:06:59,586 --> 00:07:02,631
-All right, well, who's the best superhero?
-Shh!

114
00:07:02,839 --> 00:07:06,593
You can't ask a question like that in here.
Are you trying to start a rumble?

115
00:07:08,178 --> 00:07:09,888
Well, what do you recommend?

116
00:07:10,096 --> 00:07:13,308
Well, uh, let's see, you've got your basic
clean-cut good guys:

117
00:07:13,516 --> 00:07:16,144
Superman, Spider-Man, Captain America.

118
00:07:16,353 --> 00:07:18,313
Then you have your darker anti-heroes:

119
00:07:18,521 --> 00:07:20,565
Your Batman, your Wolverine, Punisher.

120
00:07:20,815 --> 00:07:22,817
Ooh, I do love a bad boy.

121
00:07:24,819 --> 00:07:27,822
As evidenced by your boyfriend
and his fear of hamsters.

122
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:32,702
If I were you,
I'd go for Fables number one.

123
00:07:32,911 --> 00:07:35,413
Artwork is sophisticated,
it's intelligently written...

124
00:07:35,622 --> 00:07:36,998
...and it doesn't objectify...

125
00:07:37,207 --> 00:07:40,085
-...or stereotype women.
-Oh, Thor. He's hot.

126
00:07:41,419 --> 00:07:43,630
Yeah, he kind of is.

127
00:07:48,969 --> 00:07:50,428
And we're blending...

128
00:07:50,637 --> 00:07:54,099
...and we're blending, and we're done.

129
00:07:58,979 --> 00:08:03,358
I know Mr. Data isn't supposed
to smile, but here it comes.

130
00:08:07,362 --> 00:08:09,280
Come on, guys, let's do this.

131
00:08:09,489 --> 00:08:11,741
Yeah, I'm sweating my bald cap off.

132
00:08:17,163 --> 00:08:18,623
What's our first pose gonna be?

133
00:08:18,873 --> 00:08:21,376
I say we begin with a classic
Star Trek fight scene.

134
00:08:21,584 --> 00:08:23,169
I'll set the timer.

135
00:08:29,175 --> 00:08:31,136
Sheldon, how is that a fight pose?

136
00:08:31,386 --> 00:08:33,930
Mr. Data's weapon is his mind.

137
00:08:34,139 --> 00:08:35,265
I'm wielding it.

138
00:08:43,273 --> 00:08:45,108
Phasers on the Borg!

139
00:08:48,153 --> 00:08:49,946
Charlie's Angels!

140
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:54,701
Okay, what's next?

141
00:08:54,951 --> 00:08:57,704
Now let's do some sexy glamour shots.

142
00:08:57,954 --> 00:08:59,664
I'll set the mood.

143
00:08:59,873 --> 00:09:01,875
[SCATTING]

144
00:09:08,131 --> 00:09:09,549
Yeah, nice one.

145
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:12,844
That's right.

146
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:15,638
[CAR DOOR SLAMS SHUT
THEN ENGINE REVS]

147
00:09:15,847 --> 00:09:18,183
Oh, my God.
Leonard, someone's stealing your car!

148
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:21,269
What? Hey! Hey, come back here!

149
00:09:21,478 --> 00:09:24,731
-Stop!
-Stealing is against the law!

150
00:09:27,192 --> 00:09:28,526
I don't believe this.

151
00:09:28,735 --> 00:09:30,153
Son of a bitch.

152
00:09:30,361 --> 00:09:32,197
I'll call 911.

153
00:09:32,864 --> 00:09:35,116
Oh, no, my phone is in my other pants.

154
00:09:35,325 --> 00:09:36,868
-Mine too.
-So is mine.

155
00:09:37,077 --> 00:09:38,453
Anybody got any ideas?

156
00:09:38,661 --> 00:09:42,499
Nope. The only thing left to do now
is assign blame.

157
00:09:42,707 --> 00:09:44,584
Nice going.

158
00:09:48,963 --> 00:09:52,592
What kind of a person steals
another person's car in broad daylight?

159
00:09:52,801 --> 00:09:55,178
What kind of person
leaves his keys in the car?

160
00:09:55,386 --> 00:09:57,388
Thought we agreed
it's Koothrappali's fault.

161
00:09:57,639 --> 00:09:58,723
You're right.

162
00:09:59,099 --> 00:10:01,142
Nice going.

163
00:10:02,227 --> 00:10:03,812
Car.

164
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:12,445
What is wrong with people?
Why don't they stop?

165
00:10:12,654 --> 00:10:14,030
Maybe we're better off.

166
00:10:14,239 --> 00:10:16,908
What if we were to get in a car
with a crazy person?

167
00:10:17,158 --> 00:10:19,828
Look at us, Sheldon.
We're the crazy people.

168
00:10:22,914 --> 00:10:25,667
Well, perhaps we should hold up a sign...

169
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:29,504
...that assures passing motorists
of our mental competence.

170
00:10:30,839 --> 00:10:33,550
Good idea.
Why don't you get started on that?

171
00:10:35,176 --> 00:10:36,761
Come on, let's just start walking.

172
00:10:37,011 --> 00:10:39,931
There's gotta be a gas station
or something nearby.

173
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:43,893
What, you think just because you're wearing
a captain's uniform you're in charge?

174
00:10:44,269 --> 00:10:45,895
Yes.

175
00:10:46,563 --> 00:10:48,064
All right.

176
00:10:58,908 --> 00:11:00,243
Okay, I'm done.

177
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:04,539
-How did you guys finish so fast?
-I don't know, there were a lot of pictures.

178
00:11:04,747 --> 00:11:07,959
And one page only had the word
"brakka-dooom!"

179
00:11:10,295 --> 00:11:12,714
Yeah, well, I have street smarts.

180
00:11:13,672 --> 00:11:14,883
So, what'd you guys think?

181
00:11:15,383 --> 00:11:18,052
-Well, there was a lot of action.
PENNY: Hmm.

182
00:11:18,261 --> 00:11:20,597
And the story moved along
at a brisk pace.

183
00:11:20,805 --> 00:11:24,309
It was, overall,
what's the word I'm looking for...?

184
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:25,810
-Stupid?
-So stupid.

185
00:11:27,103 --> 00:11:29,230
I don't know how Leonard
can get so caught up.

186
00:11:29,439 --> 00:11:32,734
It's crazy. They spend hours arguing
about things that don't even exist.

187
00:11:32,942 --> 00:11:34,319
-What a waste of time.
-I know.

188
00:11:34,569 --> 00:11:36,779
A hammer so heavy
that no one else can pick it up?

189
00:11:36,988 --> 00:11:38,781
[SCOFFS]

190
00:11:39,616 --> 00:11:43,745
I don't think it's heavy, I think it's some
sort of magic so only Thor can lift it.

191
00:11:43,953 --> 00:11:46,122
That makes even less sense, I mean....

192
00:11:46,331 --> 00:11:49,626
No, no, no, it doesn't.

193
00:11:50,627 --> 00:11:53,712
Thor is a god. The hammer is his.
Only he can use it.

194
00:11:53,922 --> 00:11:56,758
It's like Sheldon and his toothbrush.

195
00:11:58,134 --> 00:12:00,428
Or his thin, beckoning lips.

196
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:04,432
Okay, hang on, what if Thor's hand
is on the hammer?

197
00:12:04,641 --> 00:12:08,144
If he's touching it with his god magic,
then does that mean I could lift it?

198
00:12:08,353 --> 00:12:09,979
-No.
-Yes.

199
00:12:11,481 --> 00:12:12,857
Well, which is it?

200
00:12:13,524 --> 00:12:16,361
-Maybe we missed something.
-Let's read it again.

201
00:12:16,611 --> 00:12:18,238
-Okay.
-Yeah.

202
00:12:24,452 --> 00:12:27,664
-You want some tea?
-Good idea. I'll help you.

203
00:12:28,873 --> 00:12:31,334
-Wait, I thought we were reading?
-We are.

204
00:12:31,542 --> 00:12:35,129
We're just, uh, giving you a head start.

205
00:12:44,889 --> 00:12:46,516
I wish my mom was here.

206
00:12:47,016 --> 00:12:49,727
We could all hang out in her shadow.

207
00:12:51,562 --> 00:12:53,648
Wool pants in the desert.

208
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:56,693
I feel like I've got poached testicles.

209
00:12:58,569 --> 00:13:00,822
Oh, you poor thing, you're sweating.

210
00:13:01,030 --> 00:13:03,533
That's so much worse
than having your car stolen.

211
00:13:03,741 --> 00:13:08,496
Insurance is gonna buy you a new car.
It's not gonna de-funk my junk.

212
00:13:09,539 --> 00:13:12,834
Gentlemen, a little less bellyaching.

213
00:13:13,042 --> 00:13:16,504
We're Starfleet officers
and a member of the Borg Collective.

214
00:13:16,713 --> 00:13:19,507
Please, Sheldon, I am so not in the mood.

215
00:13:19,716 --> 00:13:21,301
Leonard...

216
00:13:21,509 --> 00:13:24,554
...all our lives
we have dreamed of finding ourselves...

217
00:13:24,762 --> 00:13:27,181
...inside one of the fantasy worlds
we love.

218
00:13:27,682 --> 00:13:29,225
And look at us.

219
00:13:29,434 --> 00:13:33,187
At this moment, we are, in fact,
a Star Trek landing party...

220
00:13:33,396 --> 00:13:36,899
...stranded in an alien
and unforgiving environment.

221
00:13:37,442 --> 00:13:41,404
Relying only on our wits,
our fortitude and our moxie.

222
00:13:41,904 --> 00:13:43,865
As long as we have those things...

223
00:13:44,073 --> 00:13:45,867
-...nothing can stop us--
MAN: Nerds!

224
00:13:52,457 --> 00:13:53,875
I hate this planet.

225
00:13:59,922 --> 00:14:01,591
It says right here on the hammer:

226
00:14:01,799 --> 00:14:05,887
"Whoever holds this hammer, if he be
worthy, shall possess the power of Thor."

227
00:14:06,095 --> 00:14:08,890
Hold on. Who decides who's worthy?
Does the hammer decide?

228
00:14:09,098 --> 00:14:10,725
-Yes!
-No!

229
00:14:12,935 --> 00:14:16,272
-It can't decide, it's a hammer.
-You said it's a magic hammer!

230
00:14:16,481 --> 00:14:18,232
Yeah, but it can't make decisions.

231
00:14:18,441 --> 00:14:21,486
If Harry Potter's wand can make decisions,
why can't Thor's hammer?

232
00:14:21,736 --> 00:14:27,075
Okay, if you're gonna start comparing wands
and hammers, I can't even take you seriously.

233
00:14:45,009 --> 00:14:46,594
Hello.

234
00:14:47,970 --> 00:14:49,430
Hello.

235
00:14:51,682 --> 00:14:53,935
Four glasses of water, please.

236
00:14:55,311 --> 00:14:56,771
Anything for you guys?

237
00:14:58,189 --> 00:15:01,150
Can I use your phone?
Our car got stolen.

238
00:15:01,359 --> 00:15:03,319
Why don't you ask Scotty
to beam you up?

239
00:15:03,528 --> 00:15:05,405
[ALL LAUGHING]

240
00:15:06,823 --> 00:15:11,702
Scotty was on the original series
and we're Next Generation.

241
00:15:12,203 --> 00:15:14,997
So joke's on you.

242
00:15:18,334 --> 00:15:20,503
We're not with him.

243
00:15:25,049 --> 00:15:27,718
You can't pick something up
in outer space.

244
00:15:27,969 --> 00:15:29,429
In space, there is no up.

245
00:15:29,637 --> 00:15:32,723
Oh, yeah? Then how does
the sun come up every day?

246
00:15:34,725 --> 00:15:37,061
Hard to argue
with those kind of street smarts.

247
00:15:42,358 --> 00:15:45,695
Leonard and Sheldon have boxes of comics.
Why don't we go look at those?

248
00:15:45,903 --> 00:15:48,531
Oh, great. And then you will see,
I am not wrong.

249
00:15:48,739 --> 00:15:51,576
If we were in outer space,
anyone could pick up the hammer...

250
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:54,537
...because it would be floating
in a weightless environment.

251
00:15:54,745 --> 00:15:58,040
Yeah, that's right,
the slow reader used science. Suck on that.

252
00:16:01,252 --> 00:16:05,840
-Was there anything valuable in the car?
-Our clothes, our wallets, phones.

253
00:16:06,048 --> 00:16:07,550
And about $300 worth of makeup...

254
00:16:07,758 --> 00:16:10,845
...so this thief could look like
anyone right now.

255
00:16:12,430 --> 00:16:15,016
Makeup? Sure.

256
00:16:17,852 --> 00:16:22,398
We're going to the Comic-Con in Bakersfield.
They have a big costume contest.

257
00:16:22,607 --> 00:16:24,775
It's cooler than it sounds.

258
00:16:26,068 --> 00:16:28,446
I don't know, sounds pretty cool.

259
00:16:30,239 --> 00:16:31,908
It is. It's not just comic books.

260
00:16:32,116 --> 00:16:35,369
They've got action figures,
toys, a Tron themed dance party--

261
00:16:35,578 --> 00:16:37,413
Okay, he gets how cool it is.

262
00:16:38,414 --> 00:16:41,542
I think I have all I need here.
You guys need me to call someone?

263
00:16:41,751 --> 00:16:44,128
I'm guessing your moms?

264
00:16:46,756 --> 00:16:48,382
Thanks, but we've got it covered.

265
00:16:48,591 --> 00:16:51,093
Okay, I just talked to my mom.

266
00:16:55,264 --> 00:16:57,558
She arranged for us to get a rental car.

267
00:16:57,767 --> 00:16:59,644
Great, we can still make it to Comic-Con.

268
00:16:59,894 --> 00:17:03,272
Are you kidding me? After all we've
been through? I just want to go home.

269
00:17:03,481 --> 00:17:06,567
Don't be like that.
Come on, Howard, talk to him.

270
00:17:06,776 --> 00:17:09,529
I'm with Leonard. I'm done.

271
00:17:09,737 --> 00:17:13,616
Fine, then I guess it's two against two.
How do we decide?

272
00:17:15,826 --> 00:17:17,453
Actually, it's three against one.

273
00:17:17,954 --> 00:17:21,749
What? Well, what about the mission?
You said we're a real-life landing party.

274
00:17:21,958 --> 00:17:23,543
Well, we're not.

275
00:17:23,751 --> 00:17:26,254
We're an imaginary landing party.

276
00:17:26,462 --> 00:17:31,342
Who had real-life garbage thrown at us
by real-life strangers who think we're idiots.

277
00:17:32,176 --> 00:17:34,845
And to tell you the truth,
I'm starting to feel like one.

278
00:17:35,638 --> 00:17:37,557
I want to go home now.

279
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:40,518
Okay.

280
00:17:41,102 --> 00:17:43,938
Did we at least rent the car
from Enterprise?

281
00:17:47,024 --> 00:17:48,985
Get it? Enterprise? Heh.

282
00:17:50,486 --> 00:17:52,863
Screw you, that's funny.

283
00:17:56,158 --> 00:17:59,537
Well, what if the Hulk picked up Thor
while Thor is holding the hammer?

284
00:17:59,996 --> 00:18:01,872
-Yeah?
-Then by the transitive property...

285
00:18:02,123 --> 00:18:05,001
...of picking things up,
Hulk picked up the hammer.

286
00:18:06,711 --> 00:18:09,505
No, Hulk picked up Thor,
Thor picked up the hammer.

287
00:18:09,714 --> 00:18:13,134
Hang on, if I go to a bar and pick
up a guy, and he picks up a girl...

288
00:18:13,342 --> 00:18:16,137
...and then we all leave together,
did I pick up the girl?

289
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,518
Did that ever happen?

290
00:18:24,395 --> 00:18:26,981
Hey, we talking about me
or are we talking about Thor?

291
00:18:34,238 --> 00:18:37,199
Well, I say that's the last time
we ever go outside.

292
00:18:37,992 --> 00:18:40,870
AMY: Look right here,
Red Hulk is picking up Thor's hammer...

293
00:18:41,078 --> 00:18:42,538
...because Thor is touching it.

294
00:18:42,747 --> 00:18:45,124
PENNY:
No, it's because they're in space!

295
00:18:45,333 --> 00:18:48,044
BERNADETTE: He's touching the strap.
AMY: The strap's part of the hammer!

296
00:18:48,252 --> 00:18:49,920
PENNY:
No, it's not!

297
00:18:50,755 --> 00:18:53,507
Are they actually
arguing about comic books?

298
00:18:53,716 --> 00:18:55,134
No, that can't be right.

299
00:18:55,343 --> 00:18:59,555
Maybe Thor's Hammer
is a new color of nail polish.

300
00:19:01,098 --> 00:19:02,516
BERNADETTE:
Then Red Hulk must be worthy!

301
00:19:02,725 --> 00:19:04,518
PENNY:
How could Red Hulk be worthy?

302
00:19:04,727 --> 00:19:06,687
BERNADETTE:
You don't know his life.

303
00:19:11,776 --> 00:19:14,945
There's only one logical explanation:
Somewhere in the desert...

304
00:19:15,154 --> 00:19:17,615
...we crossed into
an alternate dimension...

305
00:19:18,074 --> 00:19:21,911
...where the women in our lives
can finally appreciate great literature.

306
00:19:24,163 --> 00:19:26,374
If it's an alternate dimension...

307
00:19:26,582 --> 00:19:28,918
...that sounds like a job
for a landing party.

308
00:19:33,589 --> 00:19:35,758
Captain, what are your orders?

309
00:19:37,301 --> 00:19:39,303
I say we investigate.

310
00:19:42,473 --> 00:19:43,557
Wait.

311
00:19:46,143 --> 00:19:47,728
They might be hostile.

312
00:19:50,398 --> 00:19:52,233
Fine, but set them to stun.

313
00:19:52,441 --> 00:19:55,903
If we vaporize Penny, I'll never
find a girlfriend that pretty again.

314
00:20:03,494 --> 00:20:04,620
GPS:
Fun fact:

315
00:20:05,454 --> 00:20:09,207
President Eisenhower
signed the Federal Aid Highway Act...

316
00:20:09,417 --> 00:20:11,627
...from his hospital room.

317
00:20:12,128 --> 00:20:14,797
Wow, that is interesting.

318
00:20:16,298 --> 00:20:18,134
You learn something new every day.

319
00:20:19,552 --> 00:20:25,599
GPS: Say, can you name the four state capitals
that are not served by the Interstate System?

320
00:20:25,808 --> 00:20:29,019
-Ooh, another quiz?
-Yes!

